      "id": "53542cd3f2b3",
      "name": "review-failed-watch",
      "prompt": "Check the ${HERMES_KANBAN_BOARD:-my-project-dev} kanban board for blocked code-reviewer cards that need auto-resolution.\n\n## Instructions\n\n1. Query the kanban board for tasks where status=blocked AND assignee=code-reviewer.\n2. For each blocked card, read the block reason and comments.\n3. If the block reason starts with \"review-failed:\" \u2014 this is a failed code review that needs auto-resolution. Load the kanban-orchestrator skill's Automated Review-Failed Resolution playbook and follow it exactly:\n   - Read the reviewer's structured comment (findings, files, verification)\n   - Extract the fix requirements\n   - Create a new coder card with the reviewer's findings baked into the body (include branch guardrails and BASE BRANCH from the original card)\n   - Create a paired code-reviewer card with parents=[new_coder_id]\n   - Comment on and archive the old blocked reviewer card\n4. If the block reason is something else (e.g. \"needs_input\", infrastructure issue, human decision) \u2014 skip it and leave it for human triage.\n5. If a card has been resolved 3+ times already (same fix issue bouncing between coder and reviewer with no progress) \u2014 escalate: do not create another fix card, instead comment asking for human review.\n\n## Important rules\n- Always include branch guardrails in new coder card bodies\n- Always pass --branch <base-branch> on kanban_create so HERMES_KANBAN_BRANCH is set\n- Always create paired reviewer cards with parents=[coder_id]\n- Always archive the old blocked card after creating the replacement\n- Never auto-resolve non-review-failed blocks\n\nReport what you did: how many blocked cards found, how many auto-resolved, what cards were created.",
